+### Fix 1 — conservative scaffold classification + pinned in-place merge publication (crates/infrastructure/src/environment/source.rs and source/tests.rs; helpers may live in a new private submodule e.g. source/publish.rs)
+
+1. Classification (read-only), used by `inspect_checkout` for the ComfyUI destination:
+   - Inspect the destination with NON-following metadata (symlink_metadata / pinned opens). Only `ErrorKind::NotFound` means missing -> `Absent`. Any other error -> `Err(failed("The ComfyUI folder could not be inspected."))` (do not use `Path::exists()`/`is_dir()` which follow links and hide errors). Destination that is a link/junction/any Windows reparse point/non-directory -> `NotGit`.
+   - If the destination contains a `.git` entry (any type, checked non-following) -> keep the existing behaviour exactly (git rev-parse HEAD -> Repo, Git failure -> NotGit). `.git` metadata must follow checkout validation, never scaffold classification.
+   - Otherwise it is `Absent` only if it is a "directory scaffold": a bounded, non-following walk (<= 64 path components deep, <= 4096 entries total) in which every entry is a real directory - no files anywhere, no symlinks/junctions/reparse points (reject every reparse point; reuse `reject_reparse`/`identity` from crates/infrastructure/src/shared/model_paths.rs), no special entries, no entry named `.git` (case-insensitive) at any depth; any traversal error or exceeded bound -> NOT a scaffold -> `NotGit`. An empty directory is a scaffold. A destination with any user file (e.g. a model downloaded before setup) stays `NotGit` (refusal unchanged).
+   - Additionally: if a valid pending publication record (see 3) exists for this destination and the destination has no `.git`, return `Absent` so that clone_comfyui reaches the clone path, which performs recovery first. (Inspection itself never mutates.)
+2. Publication in `clone_checkout` (source.rs:234-270) when the destination exists:
+   - Destination absent (NotFound) -> whole-stage rename exactly as today.
+   - Destination is a scaffold (including an EMPTY destination — preserve it now instead of `remove_dir` + rename) -> in-place MERGE using the pinned primitives in shared/model_paths.rs (`pin_absolute`, `PinnedDirectory::child(.., false)`, `open_entry`, `identity`, `reject_reparse`, `rename_no_replace`; `SafeFileName` for every component). Never pathname `exists()` + `fs::rename`.
+   - Plan the WHOLE merge before moving anything: walk staged entries; where a destination name exists (case-insensitive on Windows) it must be a scaffold directory AND the staged entry a real directory -> recurse; where it does not exist -> one planned move of that staged entry (file or whole subtree). Validate staged names and entry types too (no links/reparse points in the stage). Refuse any collision before mutation (staged file vs existing dir, existing non-scaffold entry, etc.). Refuse any planned placement inside `custom_nodes/.disabled/.vjump-no-hooks` (that dir must stay EMPTY). Fixed refusal message: "The clone could not be published because the ComfyUI folder contains entries that conflict with the checkout. It was left unchanged." After a planning refusal nothing was moved, so the stage is deleted as today.
+   - Before the first move, durably write a publication record (see 3). Execute the planned moves with `rename_no_replace` (no-replace protects against a destination appearing after planning), moving the top-level `.git` LAST. The successful `.git` move is the COMMIT POINT: after it, delete the record and remove the remaining stage skeleton (only directories that are empty; never delete files); a failure after the commit point is logged as a warning and the publication still succeeds (never roll back a completed checkout, never claim "left unchanged").
+   - Check `ctx` cancellation between moves. On a handled I/O failure or cancellation before the commit point: roll back in reverse order, moving back ONLY entries still at their destination path whose identity equals the recorded identity; then verify the destination is again a scaffold and the stage is pristine (`git -C <stage> status --porcelain=v1 --ignored --untracked-files=all` empty and HEAD == the recorded commit, run through the existing hardened git command builder with the same ctx/retention); only then delete the stage and the record and return the original error (Cancelled stays Cancelled). If any check fails or an entry is ambiguous (missing from both places, identity mismatch), retain the stage AND the record, touch nothing else, and return the fixed message "A previous ComfyUI publication was interrupted and could not be undone safely. The ComfyUI folder and the .ComfyUI.cloning folder were left for inspection."
+3. Publication record (infrastructure-internal, serde_json is fine in infrastructure): sibling file `<parent>/.<stage_name>.publish.json`, created create-new + flushed/synced before the first move. Schema version 1; destination name; stage name; resolved commit; ordered planned moves (relative path components, kind file|dir, recorded identity); the pre-existing scaffold directories (relative paths + identities). Bounded size (refuse to plan beyond the bounds). Recovery at the start of `clone_checkout` (BEFORE the current unconditional `remove_dir_all(&stage)`, which must NOT run when a record exists): if a record exists, parse/validate it (corrupt/unknown -> retain everything, fixed "could not be undone safely" message); if the destination already has `.git` whose identity equals the recorded `.git` identity, the publication had committed -> delete the record, remove only empty stage dirs, and return the existing checkout unchanged (no re-clone); otherwise run the same verified rollback as above; on success delete stage + record and continue with a fresh clone (no reuse of the old stage); on failure return the fixed "could not be undone safely" message without deleting anything. Without a record, keep today's behaviour (delete a leftover stage, then clone).
+4. ComfyUI-Manager (`install_manager`, source.rs:510-540) shares `clone_checkout`; leave its own destination checks unchanged.
+5. Add a test-only fault injection (cfg(test) only, e.g. a field or thread-local in the publication executor) to (a) fail after N moves, (b) simulate a process kill after N moves (return without rollback, record left behind), (c) stop after the `.git` commit point before cleanup.
